course: dictionary
lesson: 1274
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word bust has many meanings, so context tells you which one to use. Read the words around it first. «I bust my bike» means you broke it, because bust is an informal word for break.
explain: A bust can also be a statue of a person's head, shoulders and chest, like the ones you see in museums and history books. Look at what the sentence is about: is it about a bike, a statue or money?
explain: In business news, «the coach company went bust» means it ran out of money and closed. Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: "closed" fits, so the meaning is right.
fact: Bust has more than one meaning, so always check the context.
fact: To bust something means to break it, as in «I bust my bike» (informal).
fact: A bust is a statue of a person's head, shoulders and chest.
fact: «Go bust» means a business runs out of money and closes.
fact: «Busted!» means «you've been caught!» (slang).
exercise: «I bust my bike on the kerb.» What does bust mean here? | *To break | To paint | To sell | To ride very fast
exercise: «A bust of the old queen stood on a tall stone stand in the museum.» What is a bust here? | A type of cake | A boat | *A statue of a person's head, shoulders and chest | A broken window
exercise: «The coach company went bust last spring.» What does went bust mean? | It got bigger and busier | It won a prize | *It ran out of money and closed | It moved to a new town
test: «Busted!» shouted Mum, when she found the empty biscuit tin. What does busted mean? | Let's go home now | Hurry up | Well done, you won! | *You've been caught!
test: Spread £100 over 100 companies, so £1 goes in each. If one company goes bust, how much is lost? | *£1 | £10 | £0 | £100
exam: Ella says «bust» always means broken. What is the best answer? | Yes, it always means broken | No, it means happy | Yes, but only in museums | *No, it can also mean a statue, or a business running out of money
